<Base title="How App Composer works" description="Understand the supported agent-operated workflow, independent app contracts, ownership, configuration, updates and safe rollback."><h1>How App Composer works</h1><p class="lede">App Composer prepares a real environment and joins independently maintained apps. It uses external Codex for execution and EmDash for its managed catalogue; it does not host a competing agent.</p><p><a href="/ready/guide">First time? Follow the step-by-step setup guide</a></p><p><a href="/architecture">Read the original visual architecture explainers</a></p><section class="section"><h2>From no idea to a live app</h2><ol><li><strong>Get Ready.</strong> The agent checks its actual workspace, GitHub and Cloudflare tools. It verifies the target and deploys the configurable Hello World reference over HTTPS.</li><li><strong>Init.</strong> Choose a useful preset and customise real fields. Export a versioned spec and a matching prompt, without credentials.</li><li><strong>Compose.</strong> Pin independent source commits, validate their declared routes, data versions and capabilities, and connect their real item/action interface.</li><li><strong>Verify.</strong> Check source identity and live interaction, not just HTTP 200 or a green build.</li></ol></section><section class="section" id="standalone-get-ready"><h2>Get Ready works on its own.</h2><p>The same reusable core powers Composer’s readiness command and an independent Node consumer. It checks the actual agent, GitHub access, Cloudflare account and HTTPS source, saves private progress and rechecks it on resume. A provider interruption stays with the agent for retry; confirmed authentication or permission problems identify the human step.</p><p>The independent consumer has redeployed the reviewed Hello reference and verified its exact source on the existing custom domain. Deployment is an explicit operation with fresh prerequisite, source, account and route checks. It cannot run an arbitrary repository or create infrastructure.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/tree/main/lib/get-ready">Read the MIT core</a> · <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/blob/main/examples/get-ready.mjs">Use the standalone example</a></p><p class="note">An explicit clone operation can prepare the maintained Hello source in a new directory from an authorised existing GitHub workspace. It preserves existing files and verifies the exact checkout. Your agent must then check that destination itself; cloning grants no upstream push permission. The supported Mac path can install missing pinned dependencies in that reviewed checkout, verify its local Wrangler and resume deployment. A repeat leaves working tools alone. Global tool installation, initial account/domain setup and fresh-user provisioning remain work to prove. The local programmatic boundary is available; a hosted API and Get Ready MCP server are not exposed yet. Composer and the dashboard extension currently hand execution to your authorised agent.</p></section><section class="section" id="hello-init"><h2>Turn the reference into a first app.</h2><p>Choose Hello in Init, enter a title and introduction, choose a theme and export the specification. For the maintained reference on an existing domain, your agent uses <code>pnpm init:hello</code> to inspect the proposed configuration, then explicitly applies it after the same Get Ready checks.</p><p>The command prepares only the local configuration. Your agent reviews the diff, commits and pushes through the independent repository’s release path, deploys and verifies the changed page. A failed readiness check leaves the configuration untouched.</p><p class="note">This path supports the authored Hello repository and an existing authorised hostname. Customer forks, first accounts and preview provisioning remain separate work. Gallery layout does not apply to Hello.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>The first composition</h2><p>Photo Guestbook owns submitted media, moderation and eligibility. Curated Showcase owns its card collection. An approved item can be selected and featured; opening its card opens the source contribution. Unavailable, hidden or deleted sources fail closed. Composer joins the routes through Worker service bindings; there are no iframes or private media copies.</p><p><a href="/catalogue">Browse the EmDash catalogue</a> · <a href="/demo">Use the live pair</a></p></section><section class="section"><h2>Updates preserve your work.</h2><p>App configuration and owner data live separately from upstream source. A candidate must keep the supported contract and data version, pass capability checks and use exact commits. Incompatible versions or new permissions are rejected before activation. Your working release stays live.</p><p>The first path uses explicit, validated promotion through repository commands. Automatic branch following and webhook-driven updates remain unshipped until their concurrency and conflict gates are proven.</p><p>Code rollback restores an executable only. It never restores an old database, republishes a hidden image or discards later submissions. Storage exports and backups have their own authorised recovery path.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>The supported boundary</h2><p>This first release is operated by an authorised agent using its own GitHub/Cloudflare runtime. Public users can browse examples, explore decisions and export a spec. They cannot launch arbitrary builds, access production credentials or moderate another owner’s collection.</p><p>Owner uploads are capped and invited. No current feature implies a public multi-tenant security guarantee, provider partnership or commercial agreement.</p><p><a href="/state">Try fictional encrypted assistant state</a> · <a href="/extension">Optional Cloudflare dashboard extension</a> · <a href="/release">Verified release evidence</a></p></section><section class="section" id="state-vision"><h2>The portable identity concept.</h2><p>Portable AI State is an independent mini-app. The original concept below describes the intended assistant identity and memory layer. The current demo uses fictional profiles only; provider sync, production key recovery and use with sensitive records are not proven.</p><figure class="concept-figure"><a href="/guides/state-vision-v1.webp"><img src="/guides/state-vision-v1.webp" alt="Original Portable AI State concept, showing a human-controlled assistant profile and proposed storage, domains and provider connections." width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption>Concept supplied by the project owner. Its storage and integration labels are design proposals, not the deployed architecture. Central game login is a separate identity use case to evaluate. <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/issues/31">Security acceptance</a> · <a href="https://github.com/loftwah/app-composer/issues/44">Shared identity follow-up</a> · <a href="/release">Current verified boundary</a>.</figcaption></figure></section><section class="section"><h2>Check a page without collecting its contents.</h2><p>Add <code>?vitals=1</code> to an app’s home page and open “Page performance”. It shows loading, interaction delay and layout shift for that load, using the self-hosted Web Vitals library. Use the app, then read the updated values. They stay in the browser, including on Portable AI State.</p><p>The developer console links to public source and app activity. Each app also has <code>/healthz</code>: a small liveness check with its deployed source revision, not a promise that storage and every user flow work. <a href="/activity">The app leaderboard</a> counts public opens and selected completed actions separately.</p><p>Cloudflare’s existing public-page analytics is a separate operator view. A single local reading does not establish the 75th-percentile field result across phone and desktop visitors. <a href="https://web.dev/articles/vitals">Read the current Web Vitals guidance</a>.</p></section></Base>
